1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,896
Everyone is a programmer
now. They type a wish, and

2
00:00:03,896 --> 00:00:05,870
the machine writes the code.

3
00:00:07,270 --> 00:00:09,510
And that worries you?

4
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:16,730
I spent years learning what
happens underneath. Memory.

5
00:00:16,730 --> 00:00:19,410
Pointers. Why things break.

6
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:27,169
Punch cards. Assembly. C.
Every step was someone

7
00:00:27,169 --> 00:00:33,170
trying to say what they
meant more easily.

8
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:37,745
But if it's easy, who will
know how it works when it

9
00:00:37,745 --> 00:00:38,240
breaks?

10
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:44,250
Someone always does. The one
who wants to.

11
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:50,365
Before you, the scribe
ground his own ink for the

12
00:00:50,365 --> 00:00:55,686
manuscript. The weaver
counted every thread of the

13
00:00:55,686 --> 00:00:59,730
shemma. They feared the new
thing too.

14
00:01:00,630 --> 00:01:05,224
And yet the craft is still
here. You are just the

15
00:01:05,224 --> 00:01:08,130
newest one in a very long
line.

16
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:14,037
Do you remember the first
time the screen said "Hello,

17
00:01:14,037 --> 00:01:15,150
World" back?

18
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:18,330
I laughed out loud.

19
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:24,492
Maybe that's the point. More
people will get to feel that

20
00:01:24,492 --> 00:01:24,960
laugh.
